SPOE variables set by coraza-spoa
---------------------------------
(option var-prefix coraza)

txn.coraza.id: string, Coraza transaction ID, always set. Equals
    unique-id when passed as a spoe-message arg, else a random
    16-letter ID generated by the agent.
txn.coraza.error: integer, set by HAProxy (set-on-error), not by the
    agent. SPOE failure reason: 1=processing timeout, 2=allocation,
    3=frame too large, 4=fragmentation aborted, 5=aborted by HAProxy,
    255=unknown, 256+N=SPOP error. Unset on success. With
    continue-on-error it is not cleared between events.

The 4 variables below are written together, and only when Coraza
interrupts the transaction. All set, or none.

txn.coraza.action: string, deny, drop or redirect. This is what the
    http-request deny and http-response deny rules test.
txn.coraza.status: integer, HTTP code required by the blocking rule,
    403 with default CRS. Informational: the code actually returned is
    the one from your "deny status ...".
txn.coraza.ruleid: integer, ID of the rule that triggered the
    interruption, typically 949110 inbound or 959100 outbound with CRS.
    Only the blocking rule, not the ones that fed the score.
txn.coraza.data: string, action parameter, target URL for redirect.
    Empty for deny and drop.

txn.coraza.anomaly_score: integer, CRS anomaly score. Always the
    INBOUND score, including on the response message. Outbound score is
    not exported. 0 without CRS.
txn.coraza.rules_hit: integer, number of attack rules matched. Counts
    only rules carrying a message with ID in 910000-959999 or
    190000-199999. Number local rules in the latter range to be counted.
txn.coraza.rule_ids: string, CSV list of the IDs counted in rules_hit.
    Requires exportRuleIDs=bool(true) in the spoe-message args,
    otherwise never set.
txn.coraza.process_time: integer, ms, SPOE latency of the LAST event
    actually processed.
txn.coraza.total_time: integer, ms, sum of SPOE latency for the stream.
    The real cost of the WAF as seen by the client.
